rge</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Levesque</surname><given-names>J-F</given-names></name><name><surname>Gass</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Pineault</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Beaulieu</surname><given-names>M-D</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Operational definitions of attributes of primary health care: consensus among Canadian experts</article-title><source>Annals of Family Medicine</source><year>2007</year><season>Jul-Aug</season><volume>5</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>336</fpage><lpage>44</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17664500</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r11"><label>11</label><element-citation publication-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hordacre</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Howard</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Moretti</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Kalucy</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><source>Making a difference: report of the 2005&#x02013;2006. Annual Survey of Divisions of General Practice</source><year>2007</year><publisher-loc>Adelaide</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Primary Health Care Research &#x00026; Information Service Department of General Practice Flinders University and Australian Government Department of Health and Ageing</publisher-name></element-citation></ref><ref id="r12"><label>12</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Rothschild</surname><given-names>SK</given-names></name><name><surname>Lapidos</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Virtual integrated practice: integrating teams and technology to manage chronic disease in primary care</article-title><source>Journal of Medical Systems</source><year>2003</year><month>2</month><volume>27</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>85</fpage><lpage>93</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12617200</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r13"><label>13</label><element-citation publication-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lissing</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Powell Davies</surname><given-names>PG</given-names></name></person-group><source>Bridging the gap</source><year>2000</year><publisher-loc>University of New South Wales</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Centre for General Practice Integration Studies</publisher-name></element-citation></ref><ref id="r14"><label>14</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Khanchandani</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Gillam</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The ethnic minority linkworker: a key member of the primary health care team?</article-title><source>British Journal of General Practice</source><year>1999</year><month>12</month><volume>49</volume><issue>449</issue><fpage>993</fpage><lpage>4</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10824347</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r15"><label>15</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oliver</surname><given-names>AL</given-names></name><name><surname>Ebers</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Networking network studies: an analysis of conceptual configurations in the study of inter-organisational relationships</article-title><source>Organisation Studies</source><year>1998</year><season>Fall</season><volume>19</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>549</fpage><lpage>84</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="r16"><label>16</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weiss</surname><given-names>ES</given-names></name><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name><name><surname>Lasker</surname><given-names>RD</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Making the most of collaboration: exploring the relationship between partnership synergy and partnership functioning</article-title><source>Health Education and Behavior</source><year>2002</year><month>12</month><volume>29</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>683</fpage><lpage>98</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12456129</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r17"><label>17</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Foster-Fishman</surname><given-names>PG</given-names></name><name><surname>Salem</surname><given-names>DA</given-names></name><name><surname>Allen</surname><given-names>NA</given-names></name><name><surname>Fahrbach</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Facilitating interorganizational collaboration: the contributions of interorganizational alliances</article-title><source>American Journal of Community Psychology</source><year>2001</year><month>12</month><volume>29</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>875</fpage><lpage>905</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11800511</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r18"><label>18</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Provan</surname><given-names>KG</given-names></name><name><surname>Milward</surname><given-names>HB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Do networks really work? A framework for evaluating public-sector organizational networks</article-title><source>Public Administration Review</source><year>2001</year><volume>61</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>414</fpage><lpage>23</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="r19"><label>19</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berwick</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A user's manual for the IOM's &#x02018;quality chasm&#x02019; report</article-title><source>Health Affairs</source><year>2002</year><season>May-Jun</season><volume>21</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>80</fpage><lpage>90</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12026006</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r20"><label>20</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bonomi</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><name><surname>Wagner</surname><given-names>EH</given-names></name><name><surname>Glasgow</surname><given-names>RE</given-names></name><name><surname>VonKorff</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Assessment of Chronic Illness Care (ACIC): a practical tool to measure quality improvement</article-title><source>Health Services Research</source><year>2002</year><month>6</month><volume>37</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>791</fpage><lpage>820</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12132606</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r21"><label>21</label><element-citation publication-type="other"><collab>Improving Chronic Illness Care</collab><article-title>Clinical practice change, ACIC survey (Assessment of Chronic Illness Care) ICIC</article-title><year>2007</year><date-in-citation>Cited 2007 Sept</date-in-citation><comment>Available from: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.improvingchroniccare.org/index.php?p=ACIC_Survey&#x00026;s=35">http://www.improvingchroniccare.org/index.php?p=ACIC_Survey&#x00026;s=35</ext-link></comment></element-citation></ref><ref id="r22"><label>22</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oldroyd</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Proudfoot</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Infante</surname><given-names>FA</given-names></name><name><surname>Powell Davies</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bubner</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Holton</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>The views of Australian GPs about providing healthcare for people with chronic illness: a qualitative study</article-title><source>Medical Journal of Australia</source><year>2003</year><month>7</month><day>7</day><volume>179</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>30</fpage><lpage>3</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12831381</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r23"><label>23</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Infante</surname><given-names>FA</given-names></name><name><surname>Proudfoot</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Powell Davies</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bubner</surname><given-names>TK</given-names></name><name><surname>Holton</surname><given-names>CH</given-names></name><name><surname>Beilby</surname><given-names>JJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>How people with chronic illnesses view their care in general practice: a qualitative study</article-title><source>Medical Journal of Australia</source><year>2004</year><month>7</month><day>19</day><volume>181</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>70</fpage><lpage>3</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15257639</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="r24"><label>24</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Proudfoot</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Infante</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Holton</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Powell Davies</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Bubner</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Beilby</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Organisational capacity and chronic disease